Simplify: \old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). But spec may accept using .equals with old value, assuming non-null. Many previous examples used .equals directly (e.g., this.query(i).equals(\old(this.query(i)))). That would cause NPE if old value null. But they didn't consider null. However spec includes null case. Could still use .equals with null? In Java, calling .equals on null throws NPE. So they purposely used .equals in earlier spec where they assumed non-null values. Here they explicitly mention null case. So we need to encode both.

Use implication: (\old(this.get(i)) == null) => (this.get(i) == null) && (\old(this.get(i)) != null) => this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))). Could combine with logical OR: (\old(this.get(i)) == null ? this.get(i) == null : this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i)))).

